<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sakalava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sakalava people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sakalava are a people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. From the 17th century their Maroserana kings built the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menabe">Menabe</a> and Boina, which used firearms bought from European traders to dominate the west coast and traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and slaves at ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Mahajanga</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> centred on royal relics (dady), and they long resisted the Merina Kingdom and later the French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sakalava.jpg" alt="Sakalava" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinonlein · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sakalava_16.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sakalava are an ethnic group on the western edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, from Toliara in the south to the Sambirano River in the north, about 6.2 percent of the island's population in 2018. They are considered a mix of Austronesian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu peoples</a>. Austronesians began settling Madagascar between 400 and 900 CE, with the earliest confirmed settlements, on Nosy Mangabe and in the Mananara Valley, dating to the eighth century, and Bantu-speaking farmers arrived in the ninth. The origin of the name is not fully known. The most common explanation is that it means people of the long valleys, though some suggest the Arabic saqaliba, meaning slaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Local tradition names the Maroserana princes, from Fiherenana, now Toliara, as the founders of the Sakalava kingdom, and the first significant kingdoms formed around the 1650s. After 1610 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriandahifotsy">Andriandahifotsy</a>, descendant of the founder Andriamisara, extended his authority north beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangoky-river">Mangoky River</a> with weapons obtained through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and his two sons carried the gains as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Mahajanga</a>. Menabe, centred on Andakabe, now Morondava, was the principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a>. The kingdom reached its widest extent between 1730 and 1760 under King Andrianinevenarivo.</p><p>Demand for slaves from Omani Arabs and later Europeans led to Sakalava raids on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>, other coastal settlements and merchant ships. Arab traders first supplied weapons in exchange for slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Mahajanga</a> was the largest port for the trade, and the Sakalava held a monopoly on it until the end of the 18th century. Their guns let a smaller population force more populous groups to pay tribute. In the late 18th century the Merina king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> bought guns and made war on them, which ended their hegemony. The Merina never annexed the last two strongholds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menabe">Menabe</a> and Boina, but ruled the central highlands until the French conquest of 1896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Sakalava society became hierarchical with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, with strata that included the Ampanzaka, the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, and the Makoa, descendants of African slaves. The king is held to be sacred and must master a force called hasina to keep his position. After death a king's spirit becomes an ancestor that can possess mediums in the ceremony called Tromba. About 60 percent of Sakalava follow Fomba Gasy, a traditional religion centred on royal ancestors, whose remains were kept by the dady lineage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> was adopted by the rulers in the eighteenth century to gain the support of the Omani and Zanzibar Sultanates against the Merina and Europeans. Catholic missions followed from Nosy Be. The Sakalava are pastoralists with large zebu herds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sakalava people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With it he took the east-coast port of Toamasina in 1817, campaigned against the coastal and southern peoples, and in 1824 captured Mahajanga, the chief port of the <b>Sakalava</b> kingdom of Boina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He married a <b>Sakalava</b> princess of Boina to secure alliances.</blockquote><a href="/state/betsimisaraka-confederation">Betsimisaraka Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sakalava</b> kingship rested on sacred royal relics and ancestor cults, while Muslim and Indian merchants ran the port trade in slaves, cattle and rice with Arabia, the Swahili coast and the Comoros.</blockquote><a href="/state/boina">Boina</a></li><li><blockquote>Andriantsoly, a former <b>Sakalava</b> king from Madagascar who had become ruler of Mayotte, ceded the island to France in 1841 in return for a pension and protection from rival Comoran sultans.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-mayotte-and-dependencies">Colony of Mayotte and Dependencies</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mahafaly kingdoms emerged in the 17th century in the dry plateau between the Onilahy and Menarandra rivers, under dynasties related to the Maroserana who founded the <b>Sakalava</b> kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/state/mahafaly-kingdoms">Mahafaly Kingdoms</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Andriandahifotsy, remembered as a great conqueror, the <b>Sakalava</b> defeated local rulers with firearms obtained from European traders.</blockquote><a href="/state/menabe">Menabe</a></li></ul></section>
